\section{System Architecture and Workflow}
Our proposed framework, the Emotional Intelligent AI, is designed to perceive nuanced emotional and cognitive states from human speech and respond with appropriate therapeutic interventions based on Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT). The system is comprised of three core components: the CACTUS dataset for generating realistic conversational data, the Zonos TTS engine for creating emotionally rich audio, and a bespoke analysis model, inspired by the "Beyond Text" architecture, for interpreting vocal cues. The overall workflow is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:dataset-generation} and detailed in the subsequent sections.

% \subsection{Phase 1: Supervised Training Data Generation}
% The foundation of our model is a high-quality, emotionally relevant audio dataset. We generate this dataset through a two-step process:
% \begin{enumerate}
%     [cite_start]\item \textbf{Dialogue Script Selection:} We begin by selecting structured, multi-turn counseling dialogue scripts from the CACTUS dataset[cite: 47]. These scripts provide rich contextual and emotional scenarios, including specific negative thought patterns defined by CBT.
%     \item \textbf{Audio Synthesis:} The selected text-based dialogues are then synthesized into audible speech using the Zonos TTS engine. This process creates a large corpus of audio files where each file is precisely labeled with its corresponding cognitive-emotional state (e.g., catastrophizing, hopelessness) derived from the source CACTUS data. This resulting dataset serves as the ground truth for training our detection model.
% \end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase 1: Dialogue Generation and Audio Synthesis}

The foundation of our dataset is a collection of 100 multi-turn CBT counseling dialogues per experimental cell (four therapist conditions across four LLM backbones, all drawing on the same shared problem seeds), each consisting of approximately 10 client--therapist exchanges, generated through an LLM-driven pipeline with controlled vocal synthesis.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Turn-by-Turn Dialogue Generation.}
    We simulate counseling sessions using a dual-LLM agentic role-play setup, following the structural principles of the CACTUS framework \cite{lee2024cactus}. A Client LLM (configured with a resist persona) and a Therapist LLM interact turn by turn; in the multi-backbone experiments the same backbone generates both sides. In each turn, the client generates an utterance in response to the conversation so far; it maintains its own conversational memory through a role-reversed message history (its prior utterances as assistant messages, the therapist's replies as user messages), and the therapist generates a CBT-oriented reply from the full dialogue history. The client persona introduces deliberate resistance, skepticism, questioning of advice, or reluctance to engage with therapeutic suggestions, to create the emotionally challenging conversational dynamics where dissonance detection is most valuable.

    \item \textbf{Vocal Parameter Assignment via Zonos Director LLM.}
    After each client utterance is generated, a separate Zonos Director LLM call assigns prosodic parameters to that utterance. The director receives the client's text and outputs a structured JSON specification containing: (a) an emotion vector with continuous values for Happiness, Sadness, Fear, Anger, and other affective dimensions (each in $[-1, 1]$), and (b) prosodic control parameters including speaking rate (10--25 range, e.g., \texttt{speaking\_rate: 15}) and pitch standard deviation (pitch\_std, 20--150 range, e.g., \texttt{pitch\_std: 100}). These director-assigned parameters specify the \emph{intended} vocal delivery and serve as control signals for the TTS engine; they are distinct from the post-hoc librosa measurements described in Phase~2, which capture the \emph{actual} acoustic realization. This step determines \emph{how} the utterance should sound, operating independently of the dialogue's textual content.

The Zonos emotion vector is adapted from Paul Ekman's (1972) theory of seven basic emotions~\cite{ekman1972universals}, with two modifications to suit the requirements of a TTS synthesis pipeline. Ekman's original seven emotions are Happiness, Sadness, Anger, Fear, Disgust, Surprise, and Contempt. The Zonos engine retains six of these and makes two key adjustments:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Contempt is omitted.} Contempt manifests primarily through facial micro-expressions and head posture (e.g., sneering, eye-rolling) rather than through distinctive vocal prosody. Its acoustic correlates are inconsistent across speakers and languages, making it a poor control dimension for TTS. Moreover, contempt is clinically low-relevance in a CBT therapeutic context: our focus is on distress-related affect, not social-moral judgment.
    \item \textbf{Neutral and Other are added.} TTS synthesis requires a calibrated baseline state against which emotional deviation can be measured; hence \texttt{Neutral} serves as the zero-emotion reference point. \texttt{Other} functions as a practical catch-all for vocal qualities that do not map cleanly to any single discrete category (e.g., mixed affect, idiosyncratic prosodic styles, or ambiguous emotional blends).
\end{itemize}

Table~\ref{tab:zonos-emotions} summarizes the mapping between Ekman's basic emotions and the Zonos TTS emotion vector.

\begin{table}[hbt!]
\centering
\caption{Mapping between Ekman's seven basic emotions and the Zonos TTS emotion vector}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Ekman (1972)} & \textbf{Zonos TTS} & \textbf{Notes} \\ \hline
Happiness & Happiness & Retained: positive-valence activation \\
Sadness   & Sadness   & Retained: low-arousal negative affect \\
Anger     & Anger     & Retained: high-arousal hostile affect \\
Fear      & Fear      & Retained: high-arousal threat response \\
Disgust   & Disgust   & Retained: revulsion, avoidance \\
Surprise  & Surprise  & Retained: high-arousal orienting response \\
Contempt  & --       & Dropped: weak vocal signature, low clinical relevance \\
--       & Neutral   & Added: TTS baseline / resting state \\
--       & Other     & Added: catch-all for unclassified affect \\
\hline
\end{tabular}%
}
\label{tab:zonos-emotions}
\end{table}

    \item \textbf{Audio Synthesis via Zonos TTS.}
    The vocal parameter specification is passed to the Zonos-v0.1-transformer TTS engine as direct control signals. Each utterance is synthesized with prosodic features, pitch, tempo, loudness, and emotional style, aligned to the intended psychological and emotional context, producing audio whose vocal delivery reflects the CBT-aligned emotional state specified by the director.

    \item \textbf{Post-Hoc Emotion Extraction.}
    After synthesis, we extract emotional representations from both channels independently: text VA via vad-bert (Valence and Arousal regressed from the transcript) and speech VA via WavLM (Valence and Arousal predicted from the raw audio waveform). These extracted values, not the director-assigned parameters, serve as the emotional metadata for all therapist generation conditions and as the input to the dissonance computation described in Phase~4.
\end{enumerate}

The resulting dataset consists of multi-turn counseling dialogues where each client utterance is paired with: (1) its textual transcript, (2) text-derived VA values, (3) speech-derived VA values, (4) vocal prosodic descriptors (pitch, loudness, speaking rate) extracted from the audio waveform, and (5) synthesized audio via Zonos. This dataset serves as the controlled evaluation ground across the four therapist generation conditions, with only the therapist's prompt differing between conditions.

