\subsection{Phase 2: Vocal Prosodic Descriptor Extraction}

For the Multimodal (Vocal-Aware) therapist condition, we extract raw acoustic features from the synthesized audio to provide the therapist LLM with natural-language contextual descriptors of the client's vocal delivery. Using the \texttt{librosa} library, we compute per-utterance acoustic measurements from the synthesized WAV file:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Pitch (F0).} Fundamental frequency is estimated via \texttt{librosa.piptrack}, yielding a pitch contour from which the mean F0 (ignoring unvoiced frames) is computed. This captures the overall pitch level of the utterance (e.g., higher pitch for anxious or distressed speech, lower pitch for flat or depressed delivery).

    \item \textbf{Loudness (RMS).} Root-mean-square energy is computed via \linebreak[0]\texttt{librosa.feature.rms}, providing a measure of vocal intensity and dynamic range that reflects how forcefully or softly the speaker delivers the utterance.

    \item \textbf{Speaking Rate.} Approximated as the number of words in the transcript divided by the audio duration in seconds, providing a coarse measure of speech tempo (slower rates often associated with sadness or fatigue; faster rates with anxiety or agitation).
\end{itemize}

These numerical measurements are mapped to human-readable natural-language descriptors using predefined threshold ranges. For example, pitch values are binned into categories (very low, low, moderate, high, very high), loudness into intensity levels (very quiet, soft-spoken, moderate volume, loud, very loud), and speaking rate into tempo descriptors (slow, moderate-paced, fast, very rapid). The resulting string, e.g., ``high pitch, soft-spoken, fast speech'', is injected as a \texttt{vocal\_descriptors} field into the Multimodal therapist prompt alongside the text and speech VA values. This design follows SpeechCueLLM \cite{wu2025beyond}, which established that objective natural-language descriptions of prosody let LLMs exploit vocal cues without architectural change, and that such objective descriptions are more reliable than interpretive ``impression'' phrasings. One deviation is noted for transparency: SpeechCueLLM calibrates its categories with quantile-based, speaker- and gender-specific thresholds, whereas we use fixed absolute bin boundaries, since all client speech in the main experiment passes through a single synthesis pipeline (a limitation revisited in Chapter~6).

Critically, these prosodic descriptors are \emph{extracted from the synthesized audio output} using signal processing, not from the director-assigned synthesis parameters described in Phase~1. While the Zonos Director LLM specifies intended vocal characteristics as control signals for the TTS engine (e.g., \texttt{speaking\_rate: 15}, \texttt{pitch\_std: 100}), the librosa-based extraction in this phase measures the \emph{actual acoustic realization} of those parameters in the final WAV file (e.g., \texttt{pitch: 180 Hz}, \texttt{RMS: 0.06}, \texttt{speaking rate: 3.2 w/s}). This distinction is important: the director's assignment represents a desired delivery style, whereas the librosa measurements reflect what the audio actually sounds like, including any fidelity loss or unintended variation introduced during synthesis. All subsequent multimodal therapist conditions (Multimodal and Dissonance-Aware) use these post-hoc extracted descriptors in their prompts, not the pre-synthesis director parameters.


\subsection{Phase 3: Emotional Feature Extraction from Text and Speech}

To enable multimodal emotion-aware therapist generation, we extract emotional representations from both the client's text and speech independently:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Text-Based Emotion (VA).}
    Each client utterance is processed through a pre-trained VAD-BERT model that regresses continuous Valence--Arousal values from text. The raw output (originally in the 1--5 range) is normalized to $[-1, +1]$ for both Valence ($V_{\text{text}}$) and Arousal ($A_{\text{text}}$), providing a compact emotional representation of the semantic content.

    \item \textbf{Speech-Based Emotion (VA).}
    The corresponding synthesized audio is processed through a WavLM-based speech emotion model that predicts Valence ($V_{\text{speech}}$) and Arousal ($A_{\text{speech}}$) from the acoustic signal. WavLM captures prosodic patterns including pitch contour, energy, and temporal dynamics learned from large-scale emotional speech data, outputting normalized values in $[-1, +1]$.

    \item \textbf{Vocal Prosodic Descriptors (for Vocal-Aware condition).}
    In parallel, raw acoustic features, pitch mean (F0), loudness (RMS energy), and speaking rate (words per second), are extracted from the audio waveform using the \texttt{librosa} library. These numerical values are converted into natural-language contextual descriptors (e.g., ``high pitch, soft-spoken, fast speech'') and are used exclusively in the Multimodal (Vocal-Aware) therapist condition described in Phase~5.
\end{enumerate}


\subsection{Phase 3.5: Discrete Emotion Mapping via Russell's Circumplex Model}

To complement the continuous VA representation with interpretable discrete emotion labels, enabling direct comparison between the client's verbal and vocal emotional states, we map each VA pair to one of nine discrete emotion categories using Russell's (1980) circumplex model of affect \cite{russell1980circumplex}.

The circumplex model arranges emotions in a two-dimensional circular space where the angular position is determined by $\theta = \text{atan2}(A, V)$. The plane is divided into eight $45^{\circ}$ sectors, each centered on a canonical discrete emotion, plus a central Neutral region when both $|V| < 0.15$ and $|A| < 0.15$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
\toprule
\textbf{Sector (Angle)} & \textbf{Discrete Emotion} \\
\midrule
$0^{\circ}$ (centered)   & Happy (high positive valence, moderate arousal) \\
$45^{\circ}$             & Excited (high positive valence, high arousal) \\
$90^{\circ}$             & Tense / Anxious (neutral valence, high arousal) \\
$135^{\circ}$            & Angry (negative valence, high arousal) \\
$180^{\circ}$            & Sad (negative valence, moderate arousal) \\
$225^{\circ}$            & Depressed (negative valence, low arousal) \\
$270^{\circ}$            & Calm (neutral valence, low arousal) \\
$315^{\circ}$            & Content / Relaxed (positive valence, low arousal) \\
Center ($|V|,|A| < 0.15$) & Neutral \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

This mapping is applied independently to text VA (yielding \texttt{text\_emotion}) and speech VA (yielding \texttt{speech\_emotion}). A discrete \emph{emotion mismatch} is recorded when the text and speech labels disagree (e.g., text = Neutral but speech = Angry), providing a human-interpretable complementary signal alongside the continuous VA delta computed in Phase~4.

The circumplex model is applied to all conditions that receive emotional metadata: Emotion (Text-Only) maps only \texttt{text\_emotion}, while Multimodal (Vocal-Aware) and Dissonance-Aware map both \texttt{text\_emotion} and \texttt{speech\_emotion} and include the mismatch flag in their output.

Table~\ref{tab:condition-features} summarizes which emotional features are available to each therapist generation condition, highlighting the progressive addition of modalities and the single-variable isolation (dissonance signal) that differentiates the Dissonance-Aware condition from Multimodal (Vocal-Aware).

