\subsection{Phase 4: Emotional Dissonance Computation}

Following the approach of \cite{haydarov-etal-2025-towards}, we compute an emotional dissonance score by comparing the emotional signals extracted from text and speech independently. In the original formulation, dissonance is defined between two \emph{non-verbal} channels on recorded therapy videos: a segment is flagged when $\max\!\left(|V_{\text{SER}} - V_{\text{FER}}|,\; |A_{\text{SER}} - A_{\text{FER}}|\right) > T$ with $T = 0.5$, where SER and FER denote speech-emotion and facial-expression recognition, respectively. We retain the max-form threshold rule but substitute the facial channel with the text-emotion channel, because our target deployment (text-based teletherapy with cameras off) provides a verbal channel but no visual one:

\begin{equation}
    \delta_V = V_{\text{speech}} - V_{\text{text}}, \quad \delta_A = A_{\text{speech}} - A_{\text{text}}
\end{equation}

A binary dissonance flag is set when the absolute difference in either dimension exceeds a fixed threshold $\tau = 0.5$:

\begin{equation}
    \text{is\_dissonant} = 
    \begin{cases}
        \text{True}, & \text{if } |\delta_V| \geq \tau \text{ or } |\delta_A| \geq \tau \\
        \text{False}, & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}

The OR condition is logically equivalent to the max form of \cite{haydarov-etal-2025-towards}; we use $\geq$ rather than the strict $>$ of the original, a boundary convention that matters only if a delta equals $\tau$ exactly, which does not occur with continuous estimates.

When the client's vocal tone and verbal content point in different emotional directions, for example, text suggests ``I'm fine'' but voice sounds tense, flat, or highly aroused, the dissonance flag signals a potential moment of \textbf{hidden affect}, where the client may be minimizing distress, masking emotion, or expressing a more complex inner state than words alone reveal. Critically, this signal is used therapeutically rather than forensically: it guides the therapist toward gentle exploration of unspoken feelings, not toward detecting deception.


\subsection{Phase 5: Multimodal Therapist Response Generation}

We implement four therapist generation conditions to isolate the contribution of each emotional signal type. Within each backbone, all conditions use the same LLM and share the same CBT-oriented therapist identity (``Luna''), the same 2--3-sentence response budget, and the same prohibition on mentioning numeric scores. Each condition's system prompt describes exactly the emotional metadata that condition receives, and its user template injects the corresponding fields, ranging from no metadata (Baseline) to the full dissonance set (Dissonance-Aware); Appendix~C reproduces the exact prompts. Therapist responses are generated turn by turn within each multi-turn dialogue. Conversational continuity is maintained through the client LLM, which conditions each new utterance on the therapist's previous response; the therapist LLM receives the full conversation history alongside the current client utterance and its assigned emotional metadata.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Baseline (Text-Only).}
    The therapist LLM receives only the dialogue transcript. No emotional metadata is provided, serving as the lower-bound reference condition.
    
    \item \textbf{Emotion (Text-Only).}
    The therapist receives the dialogue transcript alongside text-derived VA values ($V_{\text{text}}, A_{\text{text}}$). This establishes the ``text-only + VA'' performance ceiling, measuring how much LLM-based empathy can improve from text-level emotional information alone.
    
    \item \textbf{Multimodal (Vocal-Aware).}
    The therapist receives the dialogue transcript, text VA, speech VA ($V_{\text{speech}}, A_{\text{speech}}$), and vocal prosodic descriptors (pitch, loudness, speaking rate expressed as natural language). However, \emph{no explicit dissonance computation is performed}. This condition tests whether adding vocal cues without mismatch reasoning improves therapeutic quality over text-only emotion information, serving as the text+speech baseline.
    
    \item \textbf{Dissonance-Aware.}
    The therapist receives the dialogue transcript, text VA, speech VA, and the computed dissonance signal ($\delta_V, \delta_A$, \texttt{is\_dissonant}). When dissonance is high, the system prompt instructs the therapist to gently explore potential emotional mismatch, reflecting what might be ``under the surface'' through curious, non-judgmental questions such as ``I wonder if part of you feels more scared or sad than the words suggest?'' This is the primary experimental condition.
\end{enumerate}


\subsection{Phase 6: Client Resistance Modeling and Automated Evaluation}

Drawing on the MIRROR framework for multimodal cognitive reframing therapy \cite{kim-etal-2025-mirror}, we incorporate two key design elements that are central to testing the practical value of emotional dissonance detection:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Client Resistance Persona.}
    To test whether dissonance-aware prompting improves therapeutic handling of difficult moments, the client LLM is configured with a \emph{resist persona}. The client may partially resist reframing suggestions, question therapeutic advice, express doubts, or articulate obstacles to change (e.g., ``I don't think that will work for me,'' ``It's hard because...''). This deliberate resistance creates conversational moments where the gap between what the client explicitly says and what they implicitly feel becomes particularly salient, precisely the scenario where emotional dissonance detection should provide the greatest benefit for a therapist.
    
    \item \textbf{Automated Evaluation Protocol.}
    Following the MIRROR evaluation framework, we employ GPT-4o as a zero-shot judge to rate each full dialogue session on seven quantitative metrics spanning three evaluation dimensions:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Therapist Skills:} Understanding (0--6) and Interpersonal Effectiveness (0--6), measuring how accurately the therapist interprets client concerns and maintains a supportive, non-judgmental therapeutic relationship.
        \item \textbf{Client Alliance:} Affective Bond (1--5), assessing the degree of emotional connection, trust, and empathy fostered by the therapist.
        \item \textbf{Cognitive Therapy Rating Scale (CTRS):} Collaboration, Guided Discovery, Focus, and Strategy (each 0--6), evaluating CBT-specific therapeutic competencies.
    \end{itemize}
    Scores are averaged across 100 independently generated dialogues for each of the four therapist conditions and each of the four LLM backbones, and are complemented by per-seed paired statistics and a swap-consistent pairwise preference layer with a cross-family second judge (protocol details in Chapter~4), yielding a comparative assessment of how emotional metadata, and specifically the explicit dissonance signal, affects measurable therapeutic response quality. The full results are presented in Chapter~5.
\end{enumerate}



